// The CoreGraphics and CoreText backend: executes a display list into a
// bitmap. It takes the place of the Rust renderer's Skia canvas and of its
// shaper, font loader and image cache.
//
// Text is shaped by CoreText a run at a time, in the fonts and the order
// the Rust shaper tried them, and every cell's glyphs are put back on the
// cell's own column: see place_glyphs.
//
// What is not as Skia drew it:
// - Glyphs are rasterized by CoreGraphics with font smoothing off. Skia
//   asked for full hinting, which no macOS rasterizer does, and ran its
//   masks through its own contrast and gamma tables, which CoreGraphics
//   has no setting for: a glyph's edge pixels are not Skia's values.
// - A glyph's x is quantized as CoreGraphics does it, not to Skia's four
//   positions a pixel, and its y is not snapped to a whole pixel when a
//   mark is raised by a fraction.
// - Shaping is CoreText's, not swash's: both apply the font's ligatures,
//   contextual alternates and mark positioning, but a font's rarer
//   features need not come out the same.
// - A named family without a bold or an italic is drawn regular; Skia
//   took the family's nearest style.
// - The caches start over when they are full; the Rust ones dropped the
//   run used longest ago.
import std::array;
import std::map;
import std::math;
import std::mem;
import std::process;
import std::string;

